# CLAUDE.md

## Project: [PROJECT_NAME]

Stack: Directus + Next.js (App Router) + Trigger.dev (self-hosted)

## Architecture

- **Data Layer**: Directus at [DIRECTUS_URL]
- **Interface Layer**: Next.js App Router (Server Components, Server Actions, Route Handlers)
- **Work Layer**: Trigger.dev (self-hosted) at [TRIGGER_API_URL]: durable tasks, retries, schedules, AI calls; runs on the Trigger.dev workers, not on the Next.js host
- **Local Dev**: Docker Compose for Directus (see the `directus-dev` plugin), `npx trigger.dev dev --env-file .env.trigger.local`, `npm run dev`
- **Deployment**: see project deployment plugin (e.g. `dokploy-dev`, `vercel-dev`); tasks are deployed separately with the Trigger.dev CLI

## Key Files

- `lib/directus.ts` - Directus SDK client, server-only
- `lib/content.ts` - every Directus read of the pages, tagged with its collections
- `types/directus.ts` - TypeScript schema matching the Directus collections (shared with the tasks)
- `app/api/revalidate/route.ts` - expires a cache tag when a Directus Flow or a task calls it
- `app/api/directus-webhook/[collection]/route.ts` - starts a task when a Directus Flow calls it
- `trigger/` - task definitions (tasks, schedules); `trigger/lib/directus.ts` is the task-side client
- `trigger.config.ts` - Trigger.dev project config
- `.env.local` (Next.js) and `.env.trigger.local` (tasks, literal values), both out of git

## Conventions

- The SDK has no `cache` option. Directus reads go through `lib/content.ts`, tagged with the collections they read (previous caching model: `withOptions` with `next.tags`; Cache Components: `'use cache'` with `cacheTag`)
- `DIRECTUS_ADMIN_TOKEN` is server-side only (`import 'server-only'`) and belongs to a dedicated user, not an administrator. Tasks use the token of their own user
- File URLs never carry a token: public files, or the asset proxy route
- Never cache a read made with a signed-in user's token
- A Server Action is a public endpoint that takes any id: authenticate with `requireUser()` and authorize before it uses the server token or starts a task. Write with the user's own token (`withToken(session.accessToken, ...)`) or check role or ownership in code
- Delegate to a task what is slow or can fail: AI calls, third-party APIs, bulk work, anything on a schedule. A task payload carries ids (and `requestedBy` for audit), never a token and never a copy of the item
- Import a task into Next.js code as a type only (`import type { myTask } from '@/trigger/my-task'`); call `tasks.trigger()` inside the handler or action, never at module level
- A task builds its own Directus client (`trigger/lib/directus.ts`); it never imports `lib/directus.ts`, which has `import 'server-only'`
- Content a task changes is refreshed by the revalidation Flow when it lists the collection; a task that calls `/api/revalidate` itself sends the secret in the `x-revalidate-secret` header and checks the response
- Task environment variables are set in the Trigger.dev project, per environment. They do not come from the Next.js host
- `@trigger.dev/sdk`, `@trigger.dev/react-hooks` and the `trigger.dev` CLI are the version of the server
- After a schema change: update `types/directus.ts`, then run `npx tsc --noEmit` (it covers `trigger/` too)
- After a mutation: `updateTag(collection)`; content edited in Directus is expired by the Flow through `/api/revalidate`
- Liveness check for Directus is `/server/ping` (the health endpoint needs a token)

## Environment Variables

See `.env.example` (Next.js) and `.env.trigger.example` (tasks) for all required variables.
